  "_note": "Neither crossing line in no-timing-blob-ref.jsonl carries \"start\" or \"end.time\" (core.md section 5.3: crossing timing is optional and there is no separate capability for it; its presence is the declaration). otel-mapping.md section 7.3's table says that when both are absent the sink fills both the span start and the span end with the sink's own receipt time for that line and always sets mocon.crossing.timing = \"none\" to say so; the resulting span is zero-duration and, per that section, a reader MUST NOT read its duration as the crossing's duration, and its position is the sink's clock, not the host's, saying nothing about when the crossing happened relative to the execution (which ran 2026-09-16T16:00:00.000Z to 2026-09-16T16:00:00.900Z on the host's clock). A real sink's receipt time is whatever wall-clock instant it happened to read that line, which is not reproducible and not specified by core.md or otel-mapping.md. For this fixture to be byte-for-byte comparable, both crossing spans in this file use one fixed constant sink receipt time, 2026-09-16T16:05:00.000Z (1789574700000000000 unix nanoseconds), chosen only to postdate every host-clock timestamp in the source stream. A conforming sink is not required to reproduce this exact instant; a conformance check against this file MUST treat startTimeUnixNano/endTimeUnixNano on these two spans as informational rather than requiring an exact match, while still requiring mocon.crossing.timing = \"none\" and start == end (zero duration) on both. Separately: render_chart's output value is an ordinary JSON object that happens to contain {\"type\":\"blob_ref\",...}; mocon.crossing.output.value is its JSON serialization like any other object value (otel-mapping.md section 8.2) because core.md's Payload rule forbids interpreting value beyond displaying it. mocon assigns it no special payload kind. The \"_note\" key itself is not part of OTLP and is not compared by conformance checks; see otlp/README.md."
